<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<UserControl
    x:Class="Rudis.Shell.Regions.Timeline"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    FontFamily="Calibri">

    <!--
      Timeline — the region name is the handoff's, character-for-character
      (design_handoff_rudis_editor/README.md:35,129; CLAUDE.md rule 7).

      EVERY colour is a {StaticResource} token key from Theme/Tokens.xaml. A raw-hex
      literal in this file is a build FAILURE
      (MechanicalGatesTests.no_raw_hex_outside_the_token_dictionary).

      ═══ THE ONE THING THIS FILE IS ABOUT ═══

      SHELL-05: "the Timeline is a custom-drawn, viewport-culled, virtualized surface
      — NOT one control per clip". That claim is made TRUE by what is ABSENT below.
      There is no repeater, no list control, no items host, no bound item template and
      no per-clip element of any kind in this file, and there never may be: a clip is a
      rectangle in an array from TimelineFrameBuilder all the way to the GPU. The
      whole lane/clip/ruler/playhead surface is ONE SwapChainPanel driven by
      rudis_timeline.dll (crates/timeline-render).

      ⚠ The acceptance check for that paragraph is a literal grep over THIS FILE for
      the five framework type names it would otherwise have used, pinned at zero. So
      the names are deliberately not written here, not even to say they are absent: a
      use-detector that its own rationale can trip stops distinguishing "used" from
      "mentioned", and the cheap fix would be deleting the rationale rather than
      fixing the gate. Plans 52-02, 52-04 and 52-05 each recorded this trap; this is
      the same rule applied before it bit.

      ═══ WHY THE PANEL SPANS BOTH ROWS ═══

      The panel is Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="2" and the `Timeline › Toolbar` is
      declared AFTER it, so the toolbar composites ON TOP of the swapchain — the same
      ordinary-XAML-over-a-Rust-swapchain layering plan 51-04 proved for Preview.

      That is not decoration, it is a coordinate-system decision. The renderer's frame
      contract carries `header_h_px` = "how much vertical space at the top of the
      surface is not the renderer's", and 52-03's TimelineViewport measures SURFACE y
      from the top of the whole region (LaneAreaYFromSurfaceY subtracts header + ruler
      in one step). Making the panel start BELOW the toolbar instead would have given
      the model and the surface two different vertical origins, and every pointer
      coordinate in plan 52-07 would have to remember which one it was in. One origin,
      one conversion.

      ═══ WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE ═══

      · The `T` (text/captions) lane the handoff draws — `crates/core::TrackKind` is
        Video | Audio only and that crate is frozen, so it has nothing to port (D-06).
      · Any MediaBin drop target — that interaction's SOURCE region is Phase 53's
        (D-09), and a fake drop affordance would be exactly the placeholder CLAUDE.md
        rule 1 forbids. The empty state is a LABEL, not a target.
      · Rubber-band / multi-select — single primary selection this phase (D-08).

      ═══ GLYPHS: THE RECORDED TRAP ═══

      Windows 10 does NOT ship the newer Segoe symbol font WinUI templates reach for by
      default, and a private-use-area codepoint has no font fallback — so pinning that
      family renders NOTHING AT ALL: an invisible control rather than a tofu box (plan
      50-05's measured finding; `C:\Windows\Fonts` on this 19045 box holds segmdl2.ttf
      and holomdl2.ttf and nothing newer). The opposite trap also exists: a plain BMP
      pictograph may fall back to a colour emoji font. Both are avoided the way
      50-05/50-06 settled it — {ThemeResource SymbolThemeFontFamily} with Segoe MDL2
      Assets codepoints — and each glyph below is VERIFIED BY LAUNCH and recorded, per
      codepoint, in artifacts/52-06-region.md. (The absent family's exact name is not
      written in this file: its acceptance check is a use-detector pinned at zero.)
    -->

    <UserControl.Resources>

        <!-- ═══ `Timeline › Toolbar` tool tile — "equal 28x26 tiles, vertically
             centered on one line" (README:132) ═══

             An explicit, token-only ControlTemplate per the 50-05 region pattern, so
             nothing inherits a WinUI system brush (which would be raw hex by proxy and
             would make the rule-7 gate meaningless). Disabled renders `text-tertiary`
             — the handoff's own disabled treatment. -->
        <Style x:Key="TimelineToolTileStyle" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-secondary}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-control}" />
            <Setter Property="Width" Value="28" />
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="26" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{ThemeResource SymbolThemeFontFamily}" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualPrimaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource accent-bright}" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualSecondaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Fill"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}">
                            <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal" />
                                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-elevated}" />
                                            <Setter Target="Glyph.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-primary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-segment-active}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Glyph.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-tertiary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                            </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Glyph"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                              Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                              FontFamily="{TemplateBinding FontFamily}"
                                              FontSize="{TemplateBinding FontSize}"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ The add-track menu, token-only (plan 52-14) ═══

             The ZoomOverflowButton's flyout note applies verbatim here: an unstyled
             presenter inherits WinUI system brushes, which is raw hex by proxy and would
             make the rule-7 gate meaningless. So the presenter is styled from tokens and
             the ITEMS carry an explicit ControlTemplate for the same reason the tool
             tiles do — the pointer-over and pressed states are where a stock template
             reaches for a system brush, and a Setter alone cannot reach them. -->
        <Style x:Key="TimelineMenuPresenterStyle" TargetType="MenuFlyoutPresenter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-elevated}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource border-subtle}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-panel}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4" />
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="TimelineMenuItemStyle" TargetType="MenuFlyoutItem">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-primary}" />
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Calibri" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,6,10,6" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualPrimaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource accent-bright}" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualSecondaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="MenuFlyoutItem">
                        <Border x:Name="Fill"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-control}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal" />
                                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-segment-active}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-segment-active}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Label.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-tertiary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                            </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                            <TextBlock x:Name="Label"
                                       Text="{TemplateBinding Text}"
                                       Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                       FontFamily="{TemplateBinding FontFamily}"
                                       FontSize="{TemplateBinding FontSize}"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- The region root. Background is `bg-app` — the handoff's body base and the
         renderer's own clear colour, so the two agree at the seam while the surface
         is still being created. MinHeight and the toolbar band's height are applied
         in the code-behind FROM TimelineMetrics, so the handoff's numbers live in
         exactly one place (see the remarks on Timeline.ApplyHandoffMetrics). -->
    <Grid x:Name="TimelineRoot"
          Background="{StaticResource bg-app}"
          KeyboardAcceleratorPlacementMode="Hidden">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="*" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- ═══ KEYBOARD (README:140) ═══
             S = split the selected clip at the playhead · Delete / Back = remove it ·
             Escape = abandon a drag and clear the selection.

             DECLARED ON THE REGION ROOT, not on the tool tiles. Plan 50-05 paid for
             this once: an accelerator hosted on a Collapsed element never fires, and
             the tiles beside it collapse into an overflow at narrow widths — so
             button-hosted accelerators would silently stop working exactly when the
             window is small, which is the hardest kind of bug to notice.

             ScopeOwner is set in the code-behind to this same root, so these are
             SCOPED rather than window-wide. That matters because these are BARE keys:
             an unscoped `S` would fire while the user was typing into `Chat` (Phase
             54) or renaming in `MediaBin` (Phase 53). Scoping them to the region makes
             the Timeline's own tab stop meaningful — which is the unfinished focus
             story plan 52-06 handed forward.

             AND THAT IS WHY THE ROOT CARRIES KeyboardAcceleratorPlacementMode="Hidden"
             (quick 260731-k1a) — the two notes belong together, because the
             root-hosting decision above is precisely what caused the defect. The
             property defaults to `Auto`, under which WinUI renders a hover tooltip
             on the element that HOSTS the accelerator. The host here is the region
             ROOT, so resting the pointer anywhere over the Timeline floated a
             tooltip; only `S` ever showed, because it is first in the collection
             below.

             `Hidden` changes PRESENTATION ONLY. It is not IsEnabled="False" and it
             does not remove anything — all four accelerators still fire. The two are
             easy to confuse, so say it plainly for whoever reads this next: the fix
             for a stray accelerator tooltip is NEVER to delete or disable an
             accelerator. Deleting `S` here would have silently taken split with it. -->
        <Grid.KeyboardAccelerators>
            <KeyboardAccelerator Key="S" Invoked="OnSplitAccelerator" />
            <KeyboardAccelerator Key="Delete" Invoked="OnDeleteAccelerator" />
            <KeyboardAccelerator Key="Back" Invoked="OnDeleteAccelerator" />
            <KeyboardAccelerator Key="Escape" Invoked="OnEscapeAccelerator" />
        </Grid.KeyboardAccelerators>

        <!-- ═══ THE SURFACE ═══
             One SwapChainPanel. Nothing per clip, now or ever — that absence IS
             SHELL-05's first criterion, and it is checked by a grep rather than by
             review. Spans both rows; see the header note. -->
        <!-- AllowDrop is on THIS element and not inherited from `WindowRoot` (plan
             53.1-02): a drag-drop routed event only reaches an element that carries
             its own flag, so an ancestor's `AllowDrop` lets that ANCESTOR receive the
             bubbled event and confers nothing downward. Without this line the surface's
             DragOver/Drop handlers never fire at all — not "fire and reject". -->
        <SwapChainPanel x:Name="TimelineSurface"
                        Grid.Row="0"
                        Grid.RowSpan="2"
                        IsTabStop="True"
                        AllowDrop="True"
                        FocusVisualPrimaryBrush="{StaticResource accent-bright}"
                        FocusVisualSecondaryBrush="{StaticResource on-accent}"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.Root"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Timeline surface"
                        AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Content" />

        <!-- ═══ The drop-preview ghost clip (phase 53.1, owner request mid-UAT) ═══
             A translucent clip-sized rectangle showing WHERE and HOW LONG the dragged
             media would land while the drag hovers a lane — the standard NLE
             drop-preview affordance. XAML overlay rather than a renderer pass because
             it exists only during a live OS drag (a handful of frames, not the hot
             path), and `IsHitTestVisible=False` because a ghost that could steal
             DragOver from the surface beneath it would kill the very drop it
             previews. Positioned from code-behind via the SAME viewport conversions
             the drop itself resolves through — one geometry, two consumers. -->
        <Border x:Name="DropGhost"
                Grid.Row="0"
                Grid.RowSpan="2"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Background="{StaticResource accent}"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource accent-bright}"
                BorderThickness="1"
                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-clip}"
                Opacity="0.35"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                Visibility="Collapsed" />

        <!-- ═══ `Timeline › Toolbar` (README:132) ═══
             Sticky BY CONSTRUCTION: it is a separate grid row over a surface that
             never scrolls it. The ruler is sticky the same way — the renderer draws it
             at a fixed y and the vertical scroll applies only to the lane stack. -->
        <!-- The AutomationId is here so the band's 36px is UIA-MEASURABLE rather than
             asserted from source — the same reason plan 50-06 gave `Transport.Root`
             one. It is a container: it invokes nothing and takes no tab stop of its
             own. -->
        <Border x:Name="TimelineToolbar"
                Grid.Row="0"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.Toolbar"
                AutomationProperties.Name="Timeline toolbar"
                Background="{StaticResource bg-bar}"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
                BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
                Padding="11,0,11,0"
                TabFocusNavigation="Once">

            <Grid VerticalAlignment="Center">

                <!-- Left group: region tag, live timecode, the three tools. -->
                <StackPanel x:Name="LeftGroup"
                            Orientation="Horizontal"
                            Spacing="{StaticResource gap-toolbar}"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center">

                    <!-- Region tag: 10px / 800 / letter-spacing .08em / `text-faint`
                         (README:217). CharacterSpacing is in 1/1000 em, so .08em is
                         80 — the same conversion TitleBar/Toolbar already use. -->
                    <TextBlock x:Name="RegionTag"
                               Text="TIMELINE"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource text-faint}"
                               FontSize="10"
                               FontWeight="ExtraBold"
                               CharacterSpacing="80"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.RegionTag"
                               AutomationProperties.Name="Timeline region" />

                    <!-- Live timecode, 12.5px / 700 / TABULAR figures (README:132,217).
                         The mechanism is Typography.NumeralAlignment="Tabular" — plan
                         50-06's MEASURED answer, verified there against a proportional
                         apparatus control. It is NOT FontFeatures="tnum", which was the
                         guess that did not work; do not re-derive it.

                         FORMAT: HH:MM:SS:FF, the handoff's own Timeline form
                         (README:132,265) — deliberately different from
                         Transport.Timecode's hh:mm:ss.mmm. See TimelineTimecode's
                         remarks for why two regions answer two different questions.

                         No Width/MinWidth: the element must be auto-sized or a width
                         measurement of the tabular claim would be vacuous. -->
                    <TextBlock x:Name="Timecode"
                               Text="00:00:00:00"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource text-primary}"
                               FontSize="12.5"
                               FontWeight="Bold"
                               Typography.NumeralAlignment="Tabular"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Margin="6,0,6,0"
                               AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.Timecode"
                               AutomationProperties.Name="Timeline position"
                               AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Content" />

                    <!-- The three tools, LIVE as of plan 52-07: each dispatches an
                         EXISTING Command shape (SplitClip / DuplicateClip / RemoveClip)
                         through the EXISTING rudis_dispatch_command (D-12). They start
                         disabled and the code-behind enables them from the SELECTION,
                         so a tile is never offering an operation that has no subject —
                         and Split additionally requires the playhead to be strictly
                         inside the selected clip, because the domain refuses an edge
                         split and a tile that can only fail is worse than a dim one. -->
                    <Button x:Name="SplitButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                            Content="&#xE8C6;"
                            IsEnabled="False"
                            Click="OnSplitClick"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.SplitButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Split clip at playhead" />

                    <Button x:Name="DuplicateButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                            Content="&#xE8C8;"
                            IsEnabled="False"
                            Click="OnDuplicateClick"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.DuplicateButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Duplicate clip" />

                    <Button x:Name="DeleteButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                            Content="&#xE74D;"
                            IsEnabled="False"
                            Click="OnDeleteClick"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.DeleteButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Delete clip" />

                    <!-- ═══ TRACK MANAGEMENT (plan 52-14) ═══

                         The owner's one scope addition, and a PORT rather than an
                         invention: v6.0 had exactly this gesture — `#tl-add-video-track`
                         and `#tl-add-audio-track` in its timeline toolbar
                         (frontend/src/main.ts:1478-1479), plus a per-lane `✕` in the
                         gutter, both gated by a confirm when the track holds clips
                         (main.ts `removeTrackAt`). CLAUDE.md convention 8 asks for prior
                         art before an affordance is finalised; v6 is this repo's own
                         prior art for this exact gesture, and it is what is being ported.

                         WHAT IS NOT PORTED, and why: the per-lane `✕`. This region's
                         gutter is DRAWN by the renderer and has no per-lane control by
                         construction — that absence IS SHELL-05 — so a per-lane close
                         box would mean new renderer hit-test scope, which is not what a
                         gap-closure plan is for. The gesture is mapped onto the toolbar
                         instead and acts on the SELECTED clip's track (D-08 gives
                         exactly one primary selection to read). The cost, stated rather
                         than hidden: an EMPTY track cannot be removed through the UI,
                         because an empty track has no clip to select.

                         Both commands already exist in the frozen `crates/core`
                         (`Command::AddTrack` at command.rs:393, `RemoveTrack` at :396),
                         both are undoable, and both travel the EXISTING
                         `rudis_dispatch_command` route the five clip edits use — no new
                         FFI export (D-12). `Ctrl+Z` is the `Toolbar` region's
                         accelerator and stays there; no second owner is declared here.

                         ONE tile with two MENU ITEMS rather than v6's two buttons, so
                         the toolbar keeps the handoff's tile rhythm while the two
                         ACTIONS are preserved exactly. -->
                    <Button x:Name="AddTrackButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                            Content="&#xE710;"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.AddTrackButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Add track">
                        <Button.Flyout>
                            <MenuFlyout Placement="Bottom"
                                        MenuFlyoutPresenterStyle="{StaticResource TimelineMenuPresenterStyle}">
                                <MenuFlyoutItem x:Name="AddVideoTrackItem"
                                                Style="{StaticResource TimelineMenuItemStyle}"
                                                Text="Add video track"
                                                Click="OnAddVideoTrackClick"
                                                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.AddVideoTrackItem"
                                                AutomationProperties.Name="Add video track" />
                                <MenuFlyoutItem x:Name="AddAudioTrackItem"
                                                Style="{StaticResource TimelineMenuItemStyle}"
                                                Text="Add audio track"
                                                Click="OnAddAudioTrackClick"
                                                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.AddAudioTrackItem"
                                                AutomationProperties.Name="Add audio track" />
                            </MenuFlyout>
                        </Button.Flyout>
                    </Button>

                    <!-- Starts disabled and follows the SELECTION, exactly as the three
                         tiles above it do: with nothing selected there is no track to
                         name, and a tile that can only fail is worse than a dim one.
                         The removal CASCADES, so a track holding clips asks first. -->
                    <Button x:Name="RemoveTrackButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                            Content="&#xE738;"
                            IsEnabled="False"
                            Click="OnRemoveTrackClick"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.RemoveTrackButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Remove track" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- Right group: the zoom control `- [] +` (README:132, right-aligned).
                     Below ~520px it collapses to a single overflow chevron
                     (README:176), driven by a MEASURED SizeChanged handler in the
                     code-behind and never by XAML's width-based visual-state trigger:
                     50-05 implemented that textbook arrangement in two placements at
                     three widths and it NEVER FIRED. (The trigger type's name is not
                     written here — its acceptance check over this file is a
                     use-detector pinned at zero.) -->
                <StackPanel x:Name="ZoomGroup"
                            Orientation="Horizontal"
                            Spacing="4"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center">

                    <!-- Zoom is PURE VIEW STATE: these three change px-per-second and
                         scroll and make no backend call at all, so unlike the tool
                         tiles they are unconditionally live. -->
                    <Button x:Name="ZoomOutButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                            Content="&#xE71F;"
                            Click="OnZoomOutClick"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.ZoomOutButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom out" />

                    <Button x:Name="ZoomFitButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                            Content="&#xE9A6;"
                            Click="OnZoomFitClick"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.ZoomFitButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom to fit" />

                    <Button x:Name="ZoomInButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                            Content="&#xE8A3;"
                            Click="OnZoomInClick"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.ZoomInButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom in" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- The narrow-window replacement for the zoom group. Collapsed until
                     the measured handler says otherwise. MDL2 E70D is the chevron the
                     handoff draws as a bare glyph.

                     It carries the SAME three controls in a flyout rather than being a
                     dead affordance: at narrow widths this is the only route to zoom,
                     and a chevron that opens nothing would leave the feature
                     unreachable exactly when the layout says it should still be
                     reachable (README:176). The presenter is styled from tokens for the
                     50-05 reason — an unstyled one inherits WinUI system brushes, which
                     is raw hex by proxy. -->
                <Button x:Name="ZoomOverflowButton"
                        Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                        Content="&#xE70D;"
                        Visibility="Collapsed"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.ZoomOverflowButton"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom controls">
                    <Button.Flyout>
                        <Flyout Placement="Bottom">
                            <Flyout.FlyoutPresenterStyle>
                                <Style TargetType="FlyoutPresenter">
                                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-elevated}" />
                                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource border-subtle}" />
                                    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
                                    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-panel}" />
                                    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6" />
                                    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
                                    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
                                </Style>
                            </Flyout.FlyoutPresenterStyle>
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                                <Button x:Name="ZoomOutOverflowButton"
                                        Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                                        Content="&#xE71F;"
                                        Click="OnZoomOutClick"
                                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.ZoomOutOverflowButton"
                                        AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom out" />
                                <Button x:Name="ZoomFitOverflowButton"
                                        Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                                        Content="&#xE9A6;"
                                        Click="OnZoomFitClick"
                                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.ZoomFitOverflowButton"
                                        AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom to fit" />
                                <Button x:Name="ZoomInOverflowButton"
                                        Style="{StaticResource TimelineToolTileStyle}"
                                        Content="&#xE8A3;"
                                        Click="OnZoomInClick"
                                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.ZoomInOverflowButton"
                                        AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom in" />
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Flyout>
                    </Button.Flyout>
                </Button>
            </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ═══ The live duration readout (README:140) ═══
             The dragging/trimming tooltip. It follows the GHOST, not the pointer, so it
             reads as a property of the clip being edited rather than as a cursor
             decoration — and it is positioned in LOGICAL px from the same GhostRect the
             renderer draws, so the two cannot disagree.

             IsHitTestVisible="False" is load-bearing, not tidiness: this sits over the
             SwapChainPanel in row 1, and without it the readout would swallow the very
             pointer moves that are updating it — the drag would stop dead the moment the
             label appeared under the cursor.

             Positioned by Margin against a Left/Top alignment rather than by a Canvas:
             one attached-property write per moved frame, and no panel whose children
             could ever be mistaken for the per-clip elements SHELL-05 forbids. -->
        <Border x:Name="DragReadout"
                Grid.Row="1"
                Visibility="Collapsed"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                Background="{StaticResource bg-elevated}"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-subtle}"
                BorderThickness="1"
                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-control}"
                Padding="6,2,6,2"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                VerticalAlignment="Top"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.DragReadout"
                AutomationProperties.Name="Edit duration">
            <TextBlock x:Name="DragReadoutText"
                       Foreground="{StaticResource text-primary}"
                       FontSize="11"
                       FontWeight="SemiBold"
                       Typography.NumeralAlignment="Tabular"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.DragReadoutText" />
        </Border>

        <!-- ═══ Empty state (README:139) ═══
             The handoff's own string, as a LABEL over the surface. It is explicitly
             NOT a drop target: dragging from the MediaBin is Phase 53's interaction
             because Phase 53 owns the source region (D-09), and an affordance that
             looks droppable and is not would be worse than no affordance at all.
             The renderer still draws the lanes and the ruler underneath it. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="EmptyState"
                   Grid.Row="1"
                   Text="Drag clips from the Media Bin to start"
                   Foreground="{StaticResource text-tertiary}"
                   FontSize="13"
                   FontWeight="SemiBold"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.EmptyState"
                   AutomationProperties.Name="Timeline empty state" />

        <!-- A renderer fault must be VISIBLE and NAMED, never swallowed (the 50-06
             rule: a domain problem and a transport fault stay on two distinguishable
             paths). Carries the renderer's own status name. No red/danger token exists
             anywhere in the handoff, so this uses `text-tertiary` on `bg-elevated`
             rather than inventing a colour. -->
        <Border x:Name="StatusMessage"
                Grid.Row="1"
                Visibility="Collapsed"
                Background="{StaticResource bg-elevated}"
                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-panel}"
                Padding="11"
                MaxWidth="460"
                HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <TextBlock x:Name="StatusMessageText"
                       Foreground="{StaticResource text-tertiary}"
                       FontSize="12"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Timeline.StatusMessage"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="Timeline status" />
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
